---
/**
 * How well Arbiter works (Pass 11, F.6): a short table of the figures from
 * its own published evaluation, each with its caveat, and two sentences
 * that say what the figures do not show. Not big-number tiles. The figures
 * come from src/lib/arbiter-evaluation.ts, whose test checks every one
 * against Arbiter's files.
 */
import { ARBITER_EVALUATION } from '~/lib/arbiter-evaluation';
import { arbiterFile } from '~/lib/arbiter';
---

<div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
  <div class="overflow-x-auto">
    <table class="w-full min-w-0 border-collapse text-left text-sm">
      <caption class="sr-only">Arbiter's evaluation, from its own published results</caption>
      <thead>
        <tr class="border-b border-ink">
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Measure</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 pr-4 font-medium">Result</th>
          <th scope="col" class="py-2 font-medium">Caveat</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {
          ARBITER_EVALUATION.map((row) => (
            <tr class="border-b border-rule align-top">
              <th scope="row" class="py-3 pr-4 font-normal">
                {row.measure}
              </th>
              <td class="whitespace-nowrap py-3 pr-4 font-medium">{row.result}</td>
              <td class="py-3 text-graphite">{row.note ?? ''}</td>
            </tr>
          ))
        }
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="max-w-reading">
    The data is synthetic and n is small: these numbers show that the live path works end to end,
    not a precision estimate. The single-prompt baseline could not be measured, so no claim is made
    that Arbiter beats it; whichever calls fell back, the policy decided every case.
  </p>
  <p class="text-sm">
    <a
      href={arbiterFile('docs/EVALUATION.md')}
      class="inline-flex min-h-11 items-center underline underline-offset-4"
    >
      The method, labels and discussion, in Arbiter's EVALUATION.md
    </a>
  </p>
</div>
